   "title": "Petrol export duty cut to nil, diesel levy raised by ₹3 a litre",
   "url": "https://www.thehindu.com/business/petrol-export-duty-cut-to-nil-diesel-levy-raised-by-3-a-litre/article71649004.ece",
   "domain": "thehindu.com",
   "source": "thehindu.com",
   "published": "Sun, 01 Nov 2026 16:18:56 +0530",
   "_dt": 1793530136.0,
   "ageDays": 1.19,
   "summary": "Unlock these with Subscription The government on Saturday (October 31, 2026) slashed the export duty on petrol to nil while hiking the levy on export of diesel and aviation turbine fuel (ATF) by ₹3 per litre and 50 paise per litre respectively for the next 15-day period. There was no change in the existing excise duty rates for petrol and diesel for domestic consumption. Diesel exports would now draw a levy of ₹11.5 for every litre against ₹8.5 per litre it drew in the previous fortnight.",
   "summary_zh": "订阅即可解锁。政府于周六（2026年10月31日）将汽油出口关税降至零，同时在接下来的15天内，分别将柴油和航空涡轮燃料（ATF）的出口税每升提高₹3和50派士。国内消费的汽油和柴油现行消费税税率没有变化。柴油出口税由此前两周每升₹8.5调整为每升₹11.5。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription The government on Saturday (October 31, 2026) slashed the export duty on petrol to nil while hiking the levy on export of diesel and aviation turbine fuel (ATF) by ₹3 per litre and 50 paise per litre respectively for the next 15-day period. There was no change in the existing excise duty rates for petrol and diesel for domestic consumption. Diesel exports would now draw a levy of ₹11.5 for every litre against ₹8.5 per litre it drew in the previous fortnight. ATF would be subject to an export levy of ₹11 for every litre against ₹10.5 for every litre as mandated for the previous fortnight. The revised levy on diesel assumes significance as India spiked its exports of the liquid vehicular fuel amid a global supply crunch, particularly in Europe. According to data from real-time analytics provider Kpler, India’s diesel exports stood at 700 kilo barrels per day in October, the highest since September 2025. The government has been conducting a fortnightly review of export levies since March 27, in the backdrop of the conflict in West Asia. These are done based on the average international benchmark prices of crude oil, petrol, diesel and ATF observed since the previous review. This is to ensure the domestic availability of retail fuels by disincentivising exports. Comments have to be in English, and in full sentences. They cannot be abusive or personal. Please abide by our community guidelines for posting your comments. We have migrated to a new commenting platform. If you are already a registered user of The Hindu and logged in, you may continue to engage with our articles. If you do not have an account please register and login to post comments. Users can access their older comments by logging into their accounts on Vuukle",
   "bodyLevel": "fulltext",
