   "title": "Wike: Why I Refused to Go to Senate After Two Terms as Rivers Governor",
   "url": "https://www.thisdaylive.com/2026/10/31/wike-why-i-refused-to-go-to-senate-after-two-terms-as-rivers-governor/",
   "domain": "thisdaylive.com",
   "source": "ThisDay（尼日利亚）",
   "published": "Sat, 31 Oct 2026 01:50:57 +0000",
   "_dt": 1793411457.0,
   "ageDays": 2.56,
   "summary": "The Minister of the Federal Capital Territory (FCT), Nyesom Wike, has said that he rejected the prospect of moving to the National Assembly after completing his eight-year tenure as governor of Rivers State. Wike said he deliberately chose not to pursue a Senate seat because he believed the position could limit his ability to express his opinions freely, particularly on issues concerning the state of the nation.",
   "summary_zh": "联邦首都 Territory（FCT）部长Nyesom Wike表示，在完成担任Rivers州州长的8年任期后，他拒绝考虑进入国民议会。Wike说，他有意选择不竞选参议院席位，因为他认为这一职位可能限制他自由表达意见的能力，尤其是在涉及国家状况的问题上。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "The Minister of the Federal Capital Territory (FCT), Nyesom Wike, has said that he rejected the prospect of moving to the National Assembly after completing his eight-year tenure as governor of Rivers State. Wike, who gave this insight yesterday during the inauguration of a pedestrian bridge constructed by Dunamis International Gospel Centre in Abuja, where he also delivered a strong message on leadership, accountability and service to humanity, said he was unwilling to surrender his freedom to speak his mind for the privilege of becoming a senator. Wike said he deliberately chose not to pursue a Senate seat because he believed the position could limit his ability to express his opinions freely, particularly on issues concerning the state of the nation. The minister took a swipe at the growing tendency among former governors to seek legislative positions after leaving office, declaring he was never attracted to the National Assembly because of the restrictions he believed would come with the position. “Many governors today, when they are done, they want to go to the National Assembly, but I said that I won’t go. They asked me why, and I said I would be prevented from speaking my mind. Since I won’t be able to speak my mind, there is no need for me to go there,” he said. The former Rivers governor insisted that he preferred to occupy positions where he could freely express his views, adding that he had consistently spoken about national issues wherever he found himself. “That is true. Everybody has forgotten what this country was before now,” he added. Wike used the occasion to commend the Senior Pastor of the church, Dr. Paul Enenche, and his wife, Becky, for demonstrating what he described as practical leadership through an investment that benefits people beyond the congregation. “Watching the documentary and seeing what you have done, touching lives, not only with your soul, but talking about the welfare of other people, that is leadership,” the minister said. He argued that leadership should not be measured by titles, political positions or public declarations but by concrete achievements and the willingness to deploy available resources to improve people’s lives. According to him, individuals entrusted with authority must be prepared to account for their stewardship, insisting that public office holders should be able to point to tangible contributions made during their time in office. Wike also urged Nigerians to look beyond social media narratives when assessing leaders, stressing the need to evaluate their performance through verifiable accomplishments. He encouraged religious institutions, corporate organisations and individuals to complement government efforts by investing in projects that address pressing community needs. Earlier, Enenche said the church’s commitment to humanity required practical demonstrations of love through giving, sacrifice and service to society. Founded on January 22, 1995, THISDAY is published by THISDAY NEWSPAPERS LTD., 35 Creek Road Apapa, Lagos, Nigeria with offices in 36 states of Nigeria , the Federal Capital Territory and around the world. It is Nigeria’s most authoritative news media available on all platforms for the political, business, professional and diplomatic elite and broader middle classes while serving as the meeting point of new ideas, culture and technology for the aspirationals and millennials. The newspaper is a public trust dedicated to the pursuit of truth and reason covering a range of issues from breaking news to politics, business, the markets, the arts, sports and community to the crossroads of people and society. You can email us at: hello@thisdaylive.com or visit our contact us page",
